<div id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Practically, the category study shows the generalization of TrustJudge. The likelihood-aware aggregation and PPL-based method are robust to task type, driving down inconsistencies nearly everywhere. The distribution-sensitive scoring is competitive overall and tends to be strongest where outputs are diverse and rubric-driven (coding, reasoning, writing).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Theoretical Derivation</h2>

<section id="A8.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">H.1 </span>Theoretical Analysis of Distribution-Sensitive Scoring</h3>

<div id="A8.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the LLM-as-a-Judge paradigm, a judge model <math id="A8.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> assesses a given response <math id="A8.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. The model’s internal assessment can be conceptualized as a conditional probability distribution over a discrete set of possible scores <math id="A8.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta=\{s_{1},\dots,s_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta=\{s_{1},\dots,s_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote this probability mass function (PMF) as <math id="A8.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R}(s)\triangleq P_{M}(S=s|R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R}(s)\triangleq P_{M}(S=s|R)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A8.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a random variable representing the score. The uncertainty or ambiguity in this assessment is captured by the conditional entropy:</p>
<table id="A8.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A8.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}H(S|R)=-\sum_{s\in\Theta}p_{R}(s)\log p_{R}(s)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}H(S|R)=-\sum_{s\in\Theta}p_{R}(s)\log p_{R}(s)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional discrete scoring protocols extract a single score by taking the mode of this distribution. We define the discrete scoring function <math id="A8.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{Discrete}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Discrete</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{Discrete}}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="A8.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A8.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{Discrete}}:\Delta^{k-1}\to\Theta,\quad f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R})=\argmax_{s\in\Theta}\ p_{R}(s)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Discrete</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Discrete</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{Discrete}}:\Delta^{k-1}\to\Theta,\quad f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R})=\argmax_{s\in\Theta}\ p_{R}(s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A8.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A8.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A8.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-simplex representing all possible probability distributions over the <math id="A8.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> scores. This function maps a probability distribution to a single point estimate. The core issue with this approach is that the <math id="A8.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\argmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\argmax</annotation></semantics></math> operator is non-injective; it discards all information about the distribution’s shape and uncertainty (entropy), mapping distinct belief states to the same output score. This information loss is a primary source of score-comparison inconsistencies.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, our proposed distribution-sensitive scoring function, <math id="A8.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{DS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{DS}}</annotation></semantics></math>, computes the expected value of the score distribution:</p>
<table id="A8.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A8.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{DS}}:\Delta^{k-1}\to\mathbb{R},\quad f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R})=\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p_{R}}[S]=\sum_{s\in\Theta}s\cdot p_{R}(s)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></munder><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{DS}}:\Delta^{k-1}\to\mathbb{R},\quad f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R})=\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p_{R}}[S]=\sum_{s\in\Theta}s\cdot p_{R}(s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A8.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This function maps the entire probability distribution to a continuous scalar value, preserving more information about the underlying assessment. The following theorem formalizes the information preservation property of <math id="A8.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{DS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{DS}}</annotation></semantics></math> compared to the information loss inherent in <math id="A8.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{Discrete}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Discrete</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{Discrete}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A8.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem H.1</span></span><span id="A8.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Information Loss of Discrete Scoring and Preservation in Expectation)<span id="A8.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A8.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> be two distinct probability distributions over the score set <math id="A8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> representing the judge model’s assessment of two different responses, <math id="A8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="A8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{1}}\neq p_{R_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{1}}\neq p_{R_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>). The discrete scoring function <math id="A8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{Discrete}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Discrete</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{Discrete}}</annotation></semantics></math> can fail to distinguish between these two assessments, whereas the distribution-sensitive scoring function <math id="A8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{DS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{DS}}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a mechanism for their discrimination. Specifically:</span></p>
<ol id="A8.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(Information Loss):</span><span id="A8.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> There exist </span><math id="A8.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{1}}\neq p_{R_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{1}}\neq p_{R_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with different conditional entropies, </span><math id="A8.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(S|R_{1})\neq H(S|R_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(S|R_{1})\neq H(S|R_{2})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, such that their discrete scores are identical: </span><math id="A8.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R_{1}})=f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Discrete</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Discrete</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R_{1}})=f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(Information Preservation):</span><span id="A8.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For the same distributions </span><math id="A8.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="A8.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> constructed in (1), their distribution-sensitive scores are distinct: </span><math id="A8.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{1}})\neq f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DS</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DS</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{1}})\neq f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A8.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We will prove the theorem by formal symbolic construction.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let the score set be <math id="A8.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us choose two distinct scores <math id="A8.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{m},s_{a}\in\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{m},s_{a}\in\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A8.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{m}\neq s_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{m}\neq s_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A8.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be the intended mode of our distributions. Further, let us choose two distinct real numbers <math id="A8.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A8.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\epsilon_{1},\epsilon_{2}&lt;0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\epsilon_{1},\epsilon_{2}&lt;0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{1}\neq\epsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{1}\neq\epsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The condition <math id="A8.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&lt;0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&lt;0.5</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that <math id="A8.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\epsilon&gt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\epsilon&gt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, which will be necessary to establish <math id="A8.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> as the unique mode. The condition <math id="A8.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{1}\neq\epsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{1}\neq\epsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures the resulting distributions are distinct.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider two responses, <math id="A8.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which elicit two different internal belief distributions from the judge model, defined as follows:</span></p>
<ol id="A8.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A8.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A8.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A8.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a probability mass function (PMF) where the probability mass is concentrated on <math id="A8.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A8.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A8.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{1}}(s)=\begin{cases}1-\epsilon_{1}&amp;\text{if }s=s_{m}\\
\epsilon_{1}&amp;\text{if }s=s_{a}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{1}}(s)=\begin{cases}1-\epsilon_{1}&amp;\text{if }s=s_{m}\\
\epsilon_{1}&amp;\text{if }s=s_{a}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A8.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A8.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a second, distinct PMF, also concentrated on <math id="A8.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> but with a different balance:</p>
<table id="A8.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A8.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{2}}(s)=\begin{cases}1-\epsilon_{2}&amp;\text{if }s=s_{m}\\
\epsilon_{2}&amp;\text{if }s=s_{a}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{2}}(s)=\begin{cases}1-\epsilon_{2}&amp;\text{if }s=s_{m}\\
\epsilon_{2}&amp;\text{if }s=s_{a}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A8.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A8.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{1}\neq\epsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{1}\neq\epsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A8.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{1}}\neq p_{R_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{1}}\neq p_{R_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A8.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Part 1: Proving Information Loss in <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{Discrete}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Discrete</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{Discrete}}</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply the discrete scoring function <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{Discrete}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Discrete</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{Discrete}}</annotation></semantics></math> to both distributions. By our choice of <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{1},\epsilon_{2}\in(0,0.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{1},\epsilon_{2}\in(0,0.5)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\epsilon_{1}&gt;\epsilon_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\epsilon_{1}&gt;\epsilon_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\epsilon_{2}&gt;\epsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\epsilon_{2}&gt;\epsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the mode for both distributions is uniquely <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A8.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A8.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R_{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Discrete</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R_{1}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\argmax_{s\in\Theta}\ p_{R_{1}}(s)=s_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\argmax_{s\in\Theta}\ p_{R_{1}}(s)=s_{m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A8.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Discrete</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\argmax_{s\in\Theta}\ p_{R_{2}}(s)=s_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\argmax_{s\in\Theta}\ p_{R_{2}}(s)=s_{m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, we have shown that for two distinct distributions <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is possible that <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R_{1}})=f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Discrete</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Discrete</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R_{1}})=f_{\text{Discrete}}(p_{R_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we consider their conditional entropies. The entropy of these distributions is a function of <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A8.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A8.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H(S|R_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H(S|R_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-((1-\epsilon_{1})\log(1-\epsilon_{1})+\epsilon_{1}\log\epsilon_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-((1-\epsilon_{1})\log(1-\epsilon_{1})+\epsilon_{1}\log\epsilon_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A8.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H(S|R_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H(S|R_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-((1-\epsilon_{2})\log(1-\epsilon_{2})+\epsilon_{2}\log\epsilon_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-((1-\epsilon_{2})\log(1-\epsilon_{2})+\epsilon_{2}\log\epsilon_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The binary entropy function <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(p)=-p\log p-(1-p)\log(1-p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(p)=-p\log p-(1-p)\log(1-p)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly increasing on the interval <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0.5)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we chose <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{1}\neq\epsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{1}\neq\epsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> within this interval, it follows that <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(S|R_{1})\neq H(S|R_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(S|R_{1})\neq H(S|R_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. This confirms that <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{Discrete}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Discrete</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{Discrete}}</annotation></semantics></math> maps distributions with different levels of uncertainty to the same output, thereby losing information. This proves the first part of the theorem.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Part 2: Proving Information Preservation in <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{DS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{DS}}</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we apply the distribution-sensitive scoring function <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{DS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{DS}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the same distributions <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{R_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{R_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A8.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A8.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{1}})=\mathbb{E}[S|R_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{1}})=\mathbb{E}[S|R_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{s\in\Theta}s\cdot p_{R_{1}}(s)=s_{m}(1-\epsilon_{1})+s_{a}(\epsilon_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{s\in\Theta}s\cdot p_{R_{1}}(s)=s_{m}(1-\epsilon_{1})+s_{a}(\epsilon_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A8.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{2}})=\mathbb{E}[S|R_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{2}})=\mathbb{E}[S|R_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{s\in\Theta}s\cdot p_{R_{2}}(s)=s_{m}(1-\epsilon_{2})+s_{a}(\epsilon_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{s\in\Theta}s\cdot p_{R_{2}}(s)=s_{m}(1-\epsilon_{2})+s_{a}(\epsilon_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate that their scores are distinct, let us assume for contradiction that they are equal:</p>
<table id="A8.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A8.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{1}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A8.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{m}(1-\epsilon_{1})+s_{a}(\epsilon_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{m}(1-\epsilon_{1})+s_{a}(\epsilon_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=s_{m}(1-\epsilon_{2})+s_{a}(\epsilon_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=s_{m}(1-\epsilon_{2})+s_{a}(\epsilon_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A8.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{m}-s_{m}\epsilon_{1}+s_{a}\epsilon_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{m}-s_{m}\epsilon_{1}+s_{a}\epsilon_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=s_{m}-s_{m}\epsilon_{2}+s_{a}\epsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=s_{m}-s_{m}\epsilon_{2}+s_{a}\epsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A8.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\epsilon_{1}(s_{a}-s_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\epsilon_{1}(s_{a}-s_{m})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\epsilon_{2}(s_{a}-s_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\epsilon_{2}(s_{a}-s_{m})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A8.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\epsilon_{1}-\epsilon_{2})(s_{a}-s_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\epsilon_{1}-\epsilon_{2})(s_{a}-s_{m})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This equality can only hold if <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{1}-\epsilon_{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{1}-\epsilon_{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{a}-s_{m}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{a}-s_{m}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. However, by our initial construction, we chose <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{1}\neq\epsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{1}\neq\epsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (so <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{1}-\epsilon_{2}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{1}-\epsilon_{2}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{a}\neq s_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{a}\neq s_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> (so <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{a}-s_{m}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{a}-s_{m}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>). This leads to a contradiction.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, our assumption must be false, and it must be that <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{1}})\neq f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>DS</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{1}})\neq f_{\text{DS}}(p_{R_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The distribution-sensitive scoring function successfully distinguishes between these two belief states, preserving the discriminative information lost by <math id="A8.SS1.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{Discrete}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Discrete</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{Discrete}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves the second part of the theorem.
∎</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">H.2 </span>Theoretical Analysis of Likelihood-Aware Aggregation</h3>

<div id="A8.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The PPL-based estimator is designed to resolve ambiguity. From an information-theoretic perspective, ambiguity in a discrete choice problem corresponds to a high-entropy probability distribution over the possible outcomes. The PPL-based method leverages an alternative signal—the generative likelihood of the rationale—to induce a more confident (lower-entropy) posterior belief for decision-making. The following proposition formalizes this concept.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A8.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition H.2</span></span><span id="A8.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Uncertainty Reduction via PPL-based Method)<span id="A8.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{1,-1,0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{1,-1,0\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of outcomes. Let <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(C|\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(C|\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> be the original outcome distribution from the judge model, and <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C|\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C|\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> its Shannon entropy. In an <span id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ambiguous regime</span>, this distribution approaches uniformity, causing <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C|\pi)\to\log|\mathcal{C}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C|\pi)\to\log|\mathcal{C}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let a new "confidence" distribution <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">conf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math> be derived from the rationales <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for each outcome <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A8.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A8.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{conf}}(k)=\frac{\exp(-\gamma\cdot\text{PPL}(J_{k}))}{\sum_{i\in\mathcal{C}}\exp(-\gamma\cdot\text{PPL}(J_{i}))}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">conf</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">PPL</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">PPL</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{conf}}(k)=\frac{\exp(-\gamma\cdot\text{PPL}(J_{k}))}{\sum_{i\in\mathcal{C}}\exp(-\gamma\cdot\text{PPL}(J_{i}))}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a scaling constant. If there exists at least one outcome <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> whose rationale has a strictly lower perplexity than another (i.e., <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists k_{1},k_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists k_{1},k_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> s.t. <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(J_{k_{1}})&lt;\text{PPL}(J_{k_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(J_{k_{1}})&lt;\text{PPL}(J_{k_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math>), then there exists a <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> such that the entropy of the confidence distribution is strictly lower than the maximum possible entropy:</span></p>
<table id="A8.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A8.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(p_{\text{conf}})&lt;\log|\mathcal{C}|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">conf</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(p_{\text{conf}})&lt;\log|\mathcal{C}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This demonstrates that <math id="A8.Thmtheorem2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{PPL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{PPL}</annotation></semantics></math> makes a decision based on a more certain signal, reducing the judgment uncertainty present in the original ambiguous distribution.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A8.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The Shannon entropy function, <math id="A8.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(p)=-\sum_{i}p_{i}\log p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(p)=-\sum_{i}p_{i}\log p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, is a strictly concave function over the probability simplex. Its unique maximum is achieved when the distribution <math id="A8.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform, i.e., <math id="A8.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}=1/|\mathcal{C}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}=1/|\mathcal{C}|</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A8.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, <math id="A8.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(p)=\log|\mathcal{C}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(p)=\log|\mathcal{C}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">In an ambiguous regime, the original outcome distribution <math id="A8.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(C|\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(C|\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> is, by definition, near-uniform. Consequently, its entropy <math id="A8.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C|\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C|\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> is close to its maximum possible value, <math id="A8.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log|\mathcal{C}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log|\mathcal{C}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, consider the confidence distribution <math id="A8.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The condition <math id="A8.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists k_{1},k_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists k_{1},k_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> s.t. <math id="A8.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(J_{k_{1}})&lt;\text{PPL}(J_{k_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(J_{k_{1}})&lt;\text{PPL}(J_{k_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> implies that the values <math id="A8.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\exp(-\gamma\cdot\text{PPL}(J_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>PPL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(-\gamma\cdot\text{PPL}(J_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math> are not all equal. As a result, after normalization, the distribution <math id="A8.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="A8.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">not uniform</span>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Because the Shannon entropy function’s maximum is uniquely attained by the uniform distribution, any non-uniform distribution must have a strictly lower entropy. Therefore,</span></p>
<table id="A8.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A8.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(p_{\text{conf}})&lt;\max_{p}H(p)=\log|\mathcal{C}|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>p</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(p_{\text{conf}})&lt;\max_{p}H(p)=\log|\mathcal{C}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A8.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A8.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C|\pi)\approx\log|\mathcal{C}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C|\pi)\approx\log|\mathcal{C}|</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="A8.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(p_{\text{conf}})&lt;H(C|\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(p_{\text{conf}})&lt;H(C|\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The parameter <math id="A8.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> controls the "peakedness" of <math id="A8.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="A8.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A8.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math> approaches a Kronecker delta function centered at the outcome with the minimum PPL, and its entropy approaches zero. Thus, for any non-trivial difference in PPLs, we can always choose a <math id="A8.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to make the decision signal arbitrarily certain.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">This proves that the PPL-based method transforms a high-entropy (ambiguous) belief state into a lower-entropy (more certain) one, thereby providing a more discriminative signal for making a final judgment.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">A desirable property of any comparison function <math id="A8.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(R_{x},R_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(R_{x},R_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetry, meaning that swapping the inputs should simply invert the outcome, i.e., <math id="A8.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(R_{y},R_{x})=-f(R_{x},R_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(R_{y},R_{x})=-f(R_{x},R_{y})</annotation></semantics></math>. Single-pass estimators often violate this property due to positional bias. The following proposition proves that our bidirectional estimator is inherently stable and symmetric by construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A8.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition H.3</span></span><span id="A8.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Symmetry and Stability of the Bidirectional Estimator)<span id="A8.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the single-pass greedy estimator be <math id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{SP}(R_{x},R_{y})=\arg\max_{k}p(k|(R_{x},R_{y}),\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{SP}(R_{x},R_{y})=\arg\max_{k}p(k|(R_{x},R_{y}),\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to positional bias, this estimator is not generally symmetric, meaning there exist pairs <math id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R_{x},R_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R_{x},R_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{SP}(R_{x},R_{y})\neq-\hat{C}_{SP}(R_{y},R_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{SP}(R_{x},R_{y})\neq-\hat{C}_{SP}(R_{y},R_{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In contrast, the bidirectional estimator <math id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">perfectly symmetric</span> for all inputs:</span></p>
<table id="A8.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A8.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{B}(R_{x},R_{y})=-\hat{C}_{B}(R_{y},R_{x})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{B}(R_{x},R_{y})=-\hat{C}_{B}(R_{y},R_{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This property makes <math id="A8.Thmtheorem3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> a stable estimator with respect to the input ordering.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A8.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let’s define the aggregated score function for <math id="A8.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{B}(R_{x},R_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{B}(R_{x},R_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A8.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(k;R_{x},R_{y})=p(k|(R_{x},R_{y}))+p(-k|(R_{y},R_{x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(k;R_{x},R_{y})=p(k|(R_{x},R_{y}))+p(-k|(R_{y},R_{x}))</annotation></semantics></math>. The decision is <math id="A8.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arg\max_{k}m(k;R_{x},R_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg\max_{k}m(k;R_{x},R_{y})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now consider the estimator for the swapped input, <math id="A8.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{B}(R_{y},R_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{B}(R_{y},R_{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. Its score function is <math id="A8.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(k;R_{y},R_{x})=p(k|(R_{y},R_{x}))+p(-k|(R_{x},R_{y}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(k;R_{y},R_{x})=p(k|(R_{y},R_{x}))+p(-k|(R_{x},R_{y}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let’s compare the score for outcome <math id="A8.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> in the first case with the score for outcome <math id="A8.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=-1</annotation></semantics></math> in the second case:</span></p>
<table id="A8.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A8.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m(1;R_{x},R_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m(1;R_{x},R_{y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p(1|(R_{x},R_{y}))+p(-1|(R_{y},R_{x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p(1|(R_{x},R_{y}))+p(-1|(R_{y},R_{x}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A8.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m(-1;R_{y},R_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m(-1;R_{y},R_{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A8.Ex21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=p(-1|(R_{y},R_{x}))+p(-(-1)|(R_{x},R_{y}))=p(-1|(R_{y},R_{x}))+p(1|(R_{x},R_{y}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p(-1|(R_{y},R_{x}))+p(-(-1)|(R_{x},R_{y}))=p(-1|(R_{y},R_{x}))+p(1|(R_{x},R_{y}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A8.SS2.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS2.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">We see that <math id="A8.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(1;R_{x},R_{y})=m(-1;R_{y},R_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(1;R_{x},R_{y})=m(-1;R_{y},R_{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. By the same logic, <math id="A8.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(-1;R_{x},R_{y})=m(1;R_{y},R_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(-1;R_{x},R_{y})=m(1;R_{y},R_{x})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(0;R_{x},R_{y})=m(0;R_{y},R_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(0;R_{x},R_{y})=m(0;R_{y},R_{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">This means that the score assigned to preference "<math id="A8.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{x}\succ R_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{x}\succ R_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>" in the first ordering is identical to the score assigned to preference "<math id="A8.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{x}\succ R_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{x}\succ R_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>" (which is outcome <math id="A8.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>) in the second ordering. Therefore, if the maximum score in the first case is for outcome <math id="A8.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the maximum score in the second case must be for outcome <math id="A8.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves <math id="A8.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{B}(R_{x},R_{y})=-\hat{C}_{B}(R_{y},R_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{B}(R_{x},R_{y})=-\hat{C}_{B}(R_{y},R_{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
